WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.125 --> 00:00:02.708
(bright music)

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:09.220
- Well, welcome back to another
episode of the Addy Hour,

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:11.260
as always it's my honor and pleasure

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:12.850
to host these conversations.

00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:15.690
Today I'm thrilled to be
able to host Juju Chang

00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:19.130
for our conversation on society, culture,

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:22.350
and being a voice for
marginalized communities.

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:24.260
So this is a name that's probably familiar

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:26.566
to all of you as listeners,
but I'm gonna go ahead

00:00:26.566 --> 00:00:29.470
and give Juju just a little
bit of an introduction.

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:30.790
So as you all likely know,

00:00:30.790 --> 00:00:33.248
Juju Chang is an Emmy
Award winning co-anchor

00:00:33.248 --> 00:00:35.500
of ABC News Nightline.

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:36.950
She also reports regularly

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:39.610
for Good Morning America and 2020.

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:42.320
Recently her decades
of reporting converged

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:45.040
in a way as she hosted
two hour long specials

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:48.070
about the rise of hate
crimes towards Asian American

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:50.600
and Pacific islander communities in 2021.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:52.770
So very important work.

00:00:52.770 --> 00:00:54.270
She is someone who's been recognized

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:56.160
for her indepth personal narratives

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:58.870
that have been set against a
backdrop of pressing national

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:01.900
and international news
from natural disasters

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:04.200
to terrorism and racial equity.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:07.040
Just to highlight a few of the
stories that she has covered.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:08.284
One was a critical examination

00:01:08.284 --> 00:01:12.940
of the controversial Remain
in Mexico immigration policy,

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:14.940
her award-winning report
trans and targeted

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:17.230
on violence against transgender women

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:19.100
of color across the country,

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:23.160
and also her extensive coverage
of the COVID 19 pandemic,

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:26.490
the science, the economic
fallout, the racial disparities,

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:30.400
the impact on hospital
ICUs and essential workers.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:32.990
Juju is someone who has
profiled numerous newsmaker

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:35.040
over the years, too many to list here,

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:38.800
but many who are influential
in lots of different spheres.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:40.490
And I also want to mention she's a member

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:44.260
of the Council on Foreign
Relations and a founding member

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:48.500
of the Board of Korean
American Community Foundation.

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:49.930
So someone who's also been invested

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:51.250
in a lot of different ways.

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:53.470
So again, I'm grateful to be
able to welcome Juju Chang

00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:56.190
to the Addy Hour podcast,
thanks for being here.

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:59.380
- I am so pleased to join you today.

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:01.300
- Excellent, wonderful to have you here.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:02.900
And again, so many important things

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:04.350
that you have on which we'll touch on

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:06.010
as we go through the episode.

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:07.360
But as my listeners know,

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:09.390
and one of the things I
think my listeners appreciate

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:10.790
is just a chance for us to be able

00:02:10.790 --> 00:02:12.920
to peel back the layers as it were.

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:14.680
So I wanted to start with
just a general check-in

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:17.310
and see how you're doing
at this point in time

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:19.750
at the end of April, 2022 with everything

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:21.830
that we're continuing to navigate.

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:23.680
How are you doing this point in time?

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:29.080
- It's so profoundly
lovely of you to do that

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:31.290
and do a mental health check in.

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:33.130
I'm okay right now.

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:34.830
It was rough going through the anniversary

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:36.680
of the Atlanta shootings.

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:38.490
I went back to Atlanta and talked

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:41.422
to victims families one year later.

00:02:41.422 --> 00:02:46.320
And you tend to absorb a
lot of the traumatic stories

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:49.271
when you're in my line of work,

00:02:49.271 --> 00:02:51.701
and there's something
called empathy trauma.

00:02:51.701 --> 00:02:54.426
I've talked to sex crimes
prosecutors who have it,

00:02:54.426 --> 00:02:59.426
and I've gone from chasing
Boko Haram in Africa

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:02.270
to the Pulse nightclub shooting,

00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:05.640
and trying to just power through
and be a good journalist.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:08.430
And don't just compact, compact, compact,

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:13.100
and then bursting into
tears at the drugstore.

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:14.846
Right now I'm okay, I've done a lot

00:03:14.846 --> 00:03:17.310
of everything from therapy

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:19.880
to giggling with my
girlfriends to meditation.

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:24.080
But I will say that some of
my African American colleagues

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:25.670
did mental health check-ins with me

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:30.313
when at the height of the AAPI Hate.

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:32.970
It was a little bit unexpected.

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:34.990
So I literally started tearing up

00:03:34.990 --> 00:03:38.430
and what everything that I'd
held back kind of poured out

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:39.860
in 30 seconds.

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:42.210
And then immediately I felt guilty

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:46.340
because for these same
African American colleagues,

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:48.684
I didn't do a mental health check in

00:03:48.684 --> 00:03:50.920
to say, I know that this hurts

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:52.950
to watch numbers of your community.

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:56.290
I know that this hurts to
have to explain, I know.

00:03:56.290 --> 00:04:00.740
And just that simple check
in meant so much to me.

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:03.690
I just assumed that my
colleagues knew how I felt

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:06.805
about their struggle and their pain.

00:04:06.805 --> 00:04:09.530
But I had never expressed it before.

00:04:09.530 --> 00:04:11.410
So I'm not only grateful to you,

00:04:11.410 --> 00:04:14.920
but I would encourage
all of us to do check-ins

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:17.772
with our friends, and
colleagues and loved ones.

00:04:17.772 --> 00:04:19.654
- So important and so well said.

00:04:19.654 --> 00:04:21.700
And I'm just impressed,

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:23.040
thankful for your level of candor

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:25.710
and impressed that the process
that you even went through

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:27.010
of letting all those emotions out,

00:04:27.010 --> 00:04:29.110
but also kind of catching
what was happening

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:31.370
in the midst of that as well.

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:33.110
And something that was
actually gonna tie back

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:35.040
into and just thinking
about the emotional toll

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.330
of the type of the work that you do.

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:38.510
I mean, it sounds like that

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:40.167
in itself may have been a journey,

00:04:40.167 --> 00:04:42.427
and way that you let things
out all in that moment.

00:04:42.427 --> 00:04:44.680
And I wonder if you've thought back

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:46.157
about how you have been processing a lot

00:04:46.157 --> 00:04:47.380
of the different things

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:48.830
that you've been walking
through up to that point.

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:50.030
And if that was a shift?

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:53.460
- Yes, I think it has been a shift

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:57.250
in that sometimes like as a journalist

00:04:57.250 --> 00:05:00.700
or maybe as a community
activist, or as a advocate,

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:02.806
you feel like you're doing something.

00:05:02.806 --> 00:05:07.806
And that goes a long way to
helping you feel overwhelmed

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:14.270
or helpless in the face
of darkness, honestly.

00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:16.700
But what I found is that when I was

00:05:16.700 --> 00:05:20.380
in Chad, Cameroon and
Nigeria following the trail

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:22.490
of Boko Haram, I was
talking to young girls

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:24.870
who had literally watched
their whole families

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:26.050
be slaughtered in front of them,

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:30.260
who were kidnapped, held as sex slaves.

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:34.023
But they were talking to
me with a flat effect.

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:38.902
And you could just see
the trauma all over them.

00:05:38.902 --> 00:05:42.840
And for some reason as an American,

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:44.330
you're kind of an arms length away

00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:46.000
from not really
understanding what it's like

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:47.650
to have your village burned down

00:05:47.650 --> 00:05:50.060
and to have your livestock slaughtered.

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:52.320
It's just a different life.

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:53.720
And, but I came back to the States

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:55.960
and literally within 10 days was

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:58.868
in Orlando outside the Pulse nightclub.

00:05:58.868 --> 00:06:02.090
And interviewing a family who
had gone there on vacation.

00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:04.280
And it happened to be an
African American family.

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:07.000
But they Googled best nightclub in Orlando

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:09.019
and they ended up at Pulse.

00:06:09.019 --> 00:06:11.800
And when I went to the home,

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:14.560
I'm seeing the aunties like cooking dinner

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:19.020
for the children who are
completely traumatized by this.

00:06:19.020 --> 00:06:23.994
And for some reason I
connected to their family more,

00:06:23.994 --> 00:06:26.000
more viscerally.

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:30.454
And I just found that the
cumulative traumatic stories

00:06:30.454 --> 00:06:32.940
was having an impact on me.

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:36.050
I could feel it, and yet
I kept holding it back.

00:06:36.050 --> 00:06:41.050
And these young women were
describing watching the gunmen

00:06:41.290 --> 00:06:43.270
in front of them walk back and forth.

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:47.300
And one looked her straight in
the eye and she played dead.

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:50.830
I mean, the presence of
mind, I don't even know.

00:06:50.830 --> 00:06:53.070
And that's a trauma that
she'll be processing

00:06:53.070 --> 00:06:54.202
for the rest of her life.

00:06:54.202 --> 00:06:58.210
And so there are so many occasions
where I talk up to people

00:06:59.242 --> 00:07:01.840
and hear their stories.

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:06.330
But in a similar vein, I was in Las Vegas

00:07:06.330 --> 00:07:07.520
with the Vegas nightclub shooting.

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:09.960
And you'll recall that was the
one where there was a shooter

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:10.940
on the 32nd floor.

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:15.340
And he had luggage case
after luggage case full

00:07:15.340 --> 00:07:16.300
of ammunition.

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:17.600
He was shooting down onto the concert.

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:18.936
And I was in interviewing
a young woman who was

00:07:18.936 --> 00:07:20.720
in the hospital the next day.

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:22.040
And she said, we heard shots run out.

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:24.140
I was with my parents. It
was just the three of us.

00:07:24.140 --> 00:07:25.387
And I started running, running, running,

00:07:25.387 --> 00:07:27.910
running until I realized
my pants were wet.

00:07:27.910 --> 00:07:31.000
And I looked down and
I thought that's blood.

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:32.747
And she'd been shot and
ran a quarter of a mile

00:07:32.747 --> 00:07:35.790
with a bullet lodged in her hip.

00:07:35.790 --> 00:07:38.300
And she said, "But
immediately out of nowhere,

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:41.070
I collapsed and a complete stranger,

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:43.510
an off-duty firefighter came

00:07:43.510 --> 00:07:45.610
and started shielding me from the bullets

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:47.550
and administering first aid."

00:07:47.550 --> 00:07:49.741
And then somebody else came
over with a makeshift gurney,

00:07:49.741 --> 00:07:52.030
one of those like concert gates.

00:07:52.030 --> 00:07:54.350
And the next day when I
interviewed the hospital workers,

00:07:54.350 --> 00:07:56.850
they were like, there were so many people,

00:07:56.850 --> 00:08:00.120
volunteers dragging
victims to the hospital.

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:01.410
And in my hospital room,

00:08:01.410 --> 00:08:03.130
the woman with the bullet lodged still

00:08:03.130 --> 00:08:04.087
in her hip said to me,

00:08:04.087 --> 00:08:08.370
"You know what I saw? I saw
one bad man in the tower

00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:11.580
and 100s of heroes here on the ground."

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:13.700
And she was like, I am so grateful.

00:08:13.700 --> 00:08:17.090
And I thought in the face
of all of that darkness

00:08:18.870 --> 00:08:22.550
you see goodness and you see hope.

00:08:22.550 --> 00:08:26.837
And so that is what I cling
to when I tell these stories

00:08:26.837 --> 00:08:30.260
of such darkness and depravity.

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:33.960
- That's such a helpful
perspective too as I imagine,

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:36.020
as you're going through all
these different components

00:08:36.020 --> 00:08:37.390
and walking through with people,

00:08:37.390 --> 00:08:39.240
and it's so helpful how she shared that.

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:41.080
'Cause even as you would
tell a story I was thinking,

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:43.260
oh, you're seeing the
hope in the midst of that,

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:45.270
the hope and the humanity and people.

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:49.200
To hear her put that in
context of one versus 100,

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:51.510
I think is so powerful.

00:08:51.510 --> 00:08:52.597
- Totally, totally.

00:08:52.597 --> 00:08:54.279
And I quote her to this
day, it's been years

00:08:54.279 --> 00:08:57.421
and I was such a powerful moment.

00:08:57.421 --> 00:08:59.087
- Really, really important.

00:08:59.087 --> 00:09:02.440
And I imagine things like
that just keep you going.

00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:04.350
But to step back even a little bit further

00:09:04.350 --> 00:09:06.250
and to give our listeners
again a peek back,

00:09:06.250 --> 00:09:07.370
I'm curious if you'd be willing

00:09:07.370 --> 00:09:09.020
to share just how you even got

00:09:09.020 --> 00:09:10.570
into this path in the first place?

00:09:10.570 --> 00:09:13.580
What motivated you to get into reporting,

00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:15.250
into journalism, into co-anchoring

00:09:15.250 --> 00:09:17.030
and to bringing these stories,

00:09:17.030 --> 00:09:19.213
these important stories
to such a larger audiences

00:09:19.213 --> 00:09:22.290
in the way that you have so effectively?

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:23.422
- Well, two things,

00:09:23.422 --> 00:09:27.433
my joke is that I basically
failed freshman calculus.

00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:34.207
I've had feedback on this like
don't buy into these rumors

00:09:34.207 --> 00:09:36.090
that these stereotypes,

00:09:36.090 --> 00:09:37.510
that women are good at math, I was like,

00:09:37.510 --> 00:09:39.360
oh no, that's not the stereotype.

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:42.350
I'm breaking the stereotype
that Asians are good at math.

00:09:42.350 --> 00:09:44.159
So I feel like...

00:09:44.159 --> 00:09:45.936
- You're safe.

00:09:45.936 --> 00:09:46.769
- Exactly.

00:09:46.769 --> 00:09:48.350
- In that comment.

00:09:48.350 --> 00:09:51.810
- But like my well meaning
immigrant parents wanted me

00:09:51.810 --> 00:09:52.728
to be an engineer.

00:09:52.728 --> 00:09:55.210
So that's what I was gonna do at.

00:09:55.210 --> 00:09:56.610
I was a freshman at Stanford

00:09:56.610 --> 00:09:59.670
and I took a required
class in political science,

00:09:59.670 --> 00:10:02.190
not because I wanted to,
but because I had to.

00:10:02.190 --> 00:10:04.250
And I ended up getting an A+ in that class

00:10:04.250 --> 00:10:07.770
at the same time that I got
a 27 out of 100 in calculus.

00:10:07.770 --> 00:10:10.296
And I thought, huh, I feel
like the universe is trying

00:10:10.296 --> 00:10:11.691
to tell me something.

00:10:11.691 --> 00:10:14.793
And that combined with the fact that.

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:19.630
I think we talk about how
representation matters

00:10:19.630 --> 00:10:21.170
and role models matter.

00:10:21.170 --> 00:10:22.770
At the time Connie Chung

00:10:22.770 --> 00:10:26.240
was a prominent Asian
American broadcaster.

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:28.240
And my mother was the one who said to me

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:31.140
that Connie Chung, you could be like her.

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:32.840
And it was that moment.

00:10:32.840 --> 00:10:35.070
Like that's why role
models are so important

00:10:35.070 --> 00:10:39.070
is because role models give
you permission to dream.

00:10:39.070 --> 00:10:40.210
And that's what happened.

00:10:40.210 --> 00:10:42.300
And it allowed me for the first time

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:45.470
to go against sort of what
my family, my culture,

00:10:45.470 --> 00:10:48.370
everyone around me was
saying that I should do.

00:10:48.370 --> 00:10:51.530
And then thirdly, I think
part of the reason why,

00:10:51.530 --> 00:10:54.929
and I really have only thought
about this more recently.

00:10:54.929 --> 00:10:57.480
But part of the reason why I
think I've always been drawn

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:01.630
to stories of marginalized
communities and the underdog,

00:11:01.630 --> 00:11:05.370
and the powerless is that
that's the way I grew up.

00:11:05.370 --> 00:11:07.970
I grew up an immigrant child from Korea.

00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:09.030
I was born in Korea,

00:11:09.030 --> 00:11:12.120
came to the Northern
California when I was four

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:13.810
and felt very much like an outsider.

00:11:13.810 --> 00:11:16.350
Like it was Silicon Valley
before it was Silicon Valley.

00:11:16.350 --> 00:11:21.150
So it was cherry orchards apricot trees.

00:11:21.150 --> 00:11:24.313
And there was no some
advanced micro devices.

00:11:26.150 --> 00:11:30.730
And I went to school thinking
that my family talked funny

00:11:30.730 --> 00:11:34.060
and my family's food smelled funny.

00:11:34.060 --> 00:11:37.540
And I was told on the playground a lot

00:11:37.540 --> 00:11:39.320
that my face looked funny.

00:11:39.320 --> 00:11:41.010
In fact, I tell the story that I used

00:11:41.010 --> 00:11:43.690
to go to sleep at night
with scotch tape on my nose.

00:11:43.690 --> 00:11:46.350
Because I wanted to wake up
and look like a California girl

00:11:46.350 --> 00:11:48.123
with like not a flat nose.

00:11:49.160 --> 00:11:54.160
So I watched my father be treated poorly,

00:11:57.230 --> 00:12:00.254
and I didn't have words to
describe what I was witnessing.

00:12:00.254 --> 00:12:03.730
Like today you would call
it racism or microaggression

00:12:03.730 --> 00:12:04.563
or whatever.

00:12:04.563 --> 00:12:06.940
But at the time I was a young girl

00:12:06.940 --> 00:12:09.187
and I just knew that it wasn't right.

00:12:09.187 --> 00:12:11.940
And I knew that it didn't feel right.

00:12:11.940 --> 00:12:13.640
And I didn't like it.

00:12:13.640 --> 00:12:18.640
And I still, I think I spent
the next 30 years seeing

00:12:19.020 --> 00:12:22.870
the world through the
eyes of that young girl

00:12:22.870 --> 00:12:25.710
who felt marginalized, who felt other,

00:12:25.710 --> 00:12:29.810
who understood what it
means to be dismissed

00:12:29.810 --> 00:12:31.549
or invisible, or all those things

00:12:31.549 --> 00:12:33.630
that happened to marginalized community.

00:12:33.630 --> 00:12:36.240
And so that's part of why I'm drawn

00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:37.650
to stories on the border.

00:12:37.650 --> 00:12:39.580
You mentioned the story
about "Remain in Mexico."

00:12:39.580 --> 00:12:42.380
I went and did stories
about pregnant mothers,

00:12:42.380 --> 00:12:45.263
bringing young children
and their unborn babies

00:12:45.263 --> 00:12:47.320
to a better place.

00:12:47.320 --> 00:12:50.580
Which is essentially what
happened to me at the age of four.

00:12:50.580 --> 00:12:52.140
So I'm seeing like four year old kids

00:12:52.140 --> 00:12:55.013
and I'm relating to them
in totally a different way.

00:12:56.430 --> 00:12:59.280
It's why I went to Honduras

00:12:59.280 --> 00:13:01.300
and did a whole series on femicide.

00:13:01.300 --> 00:13:03.850
Why Honduras was one of
the most dangerous places

00:13:03.850 --> 00:13:08.506
to be a woman and explored
why violence against women

00:13:08.506 --> 00:13:11.270
is often sexualized,

00:13:11.270 --> 00:13:12.960
it's often intimate partner violence.

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:16.010
It's often violence that
involves rampant guns

00:13:16.010 --> 00:13:17.831
and lack of accountability.

00:13:17.831 --> 00:13:20.600
And those are all things that happen

00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:22.771
in societies at different levels.

00:13:22.771 --> 00:13:27.771
And so it was a really, it
was they call it femicide.

00:13:27.870 --> 00:13:32.103
Women would disappear and no
one would be held accountable.

00:13:34.777 --> 00:13:37.230
There are so many
stories where I look back

00:13:37.230 --> 00:13:38.690
and I realize I'm drawn to them

00:13:38.690 --> 00:13:42.710
because I'm drawn to telling
the stories of the little guy.

00:13:42.710 --> 00:13:45.350
- That's really, really
powerful the way you said that.

00:13:45.350 --> 00:13:47.302
Was there a point that you feel
like you were able to start

00:13:47.302 --> 00:13:49.170
to verbalize that?

00:13:49.170 --> 00:13:52.200
I'm gonna assume that as a
four year old, probably not,

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:53.940
but there may have been
a path along the way.

00:13:53.940 --> 00:13:56.260
'Cause even as you're
talking back through it,

00:13:56.260 --> 00:13:58.980
seems like you can clearly
pick out specific instances

00:13:58.980 --> 00:14:02.410
and situations that shaped
how you look at things.

00:14:02.410 --> 00:14:05.290
But was the point where
you became more aware of it

00:14:05.290 --> 00:14:06.650
and that you were able to verbalize that

00:14:06.650 --> 00:14:09.850
and translate that into what
you were actually doing?

00:14:09.850 --> 00:14:11.720
- I think it took a really long time.

00:14:11.720 --> 00:14:13.670
I'm a little sick in the head, I guess.

00:14:18.130 --> 00:14:22.313
I was more early on
interested in making my way

00:14:23.810 --> 00:14:24.643
in the world.

00:14:24.643 --> 00:14:27.620
And so I wasn't about my identity.

00:14:27.620 --> 00:14:30.290
Like I think that's the big change.

00:14:30.290 --> 00:14:34.415
Frankly for me I was part of the shift

00:14:34.415 --> 00:14:39.020
in thinking after George Floyd's murder.

00:14:39.020 --> 00:14:41.460
I just feel like I took inspiration

00:14:41.460 --> 00:14:44.500
for my African American
colleagues who in that moment

00:14:44.500 --> 00:14:47.474
for the first time
Pierre Thomas, like Sibos

00:14:47.474 --> 00:14:49.570
and Sami some of my closest friends,

00:14:49.570 --> 00:14:52.200
and colleagues of ABC News,
who I've known for 30 years,

00:14:52.200 --> 00:14:54.670
who've never talked
about their upbringing,

00:14:54.670 --> 00:14:57.460
their lived experience
into their reporting.

00:14:57.460 --> 00:15:00.348
They started giving the
rest of the world insights

00:15:00.348 --> 00:15:03.910
into what it's like to be a
black man driving home from work

00:15:03.910 --> 00:15:05.810
and being pulled over.

00:15:05.810 --> 00:15:10.420
And so I started doing that a bit

00:15:10.420 --> 00:15:14.070
in my reporting, especially
with regard obviously

00:15:14.070 --> 00:15:16.490
to AAPI Hate to talk about,

00:15:16.490 --> 00:15:17.700
let me give you some perspective.

00:15:17.700 --> 00:15:19.717
'Cause I had done that in other areas.

00:15:19.717 --> 00:15:22.390
So like if I was doing
a story about parenting

00:15:22.390 --> 00:15:27.000
or mommy angst, I would
bring my experience as a mom

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:27.833
into the story,

00:15:27.833 --> 00:15:31.260
like what that would mean
nothing but a net gain.

00:15:31.260 --> 00:15:35.630
So I realized that really
pretty late in life

00:15:35.630 --> 00:15:39.904
that that's what my value
added was in a funny way.

00:15:39.904 --> 00:15:41.832
And so I had founded this

00:15:41.832 --> 00:15:44.150
Korean American Community Foundation,

00:15:44.150 --> 00:15:45.420
which we're about to have our gala.

00:15:45.420 --> 00:15:48.840
We raised $2 million
last year, like blossomed

00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:50.780
into this huge undertaking.

00:15:50.780 --> 00:15:54.740
But it was always something did
off camera, outside of work.

00:15:54.740 --> 00:15:57.010
And now I've brought my understanding

00:15:57.010 --> 00:16:00.010
and my identity more central.

00:16:00.010 --> 00:16:02.000
And that has been a shift for me

00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:06.751
that I don't know honestly,
if I can put pinpoint

00:16:06.751 --> 00:16:11.390
when that happened, but it
was a slow burn for sure.

00:16:11.390 --> 00:16:12.500
- Well, that's really helpful to know,

00:16:12.500 --> 00:16:16.270
and just to see your process
and journey in that as well.

00:16:16.270 --> 00:16:17.170
I mean, even to be honest,

00:16:17.170 --> 00:16:18.740
some conversations I was having today,

00:16:18.740 --> 00:16:20.730
even in terms of parallels

00:16:20.730 --> 00:16:23.430
between the African American community,

00:16:23.430 --> 00:16:25.020
the AAPI community and the things

00:16:25.020 --> 00:16:26.760
that people are walking through.

00:16:26.760 --> 00:16:29.070
But as you've mentioned, it
seems like the conversation

00:16:29.070 --> 00:16:32.370
is shifting in the sense
that there's more openness

00:16:32.370 --> 00:16:34.480
to speak about those things across group

00:16:34.480 --> 00:16:37.580
and to have some aspects of similarity.

00:16:37.580 --> 00:16:39.280
And this is a fresh thought
I'm still formulate.

00:16:39.280 --> 00:16:41.310
But one thing I've also
noticed is there seems

00:16:41.310 --> 00:16:43.690
to be some commonalities that were there,

00:16:43.690 --> 00:16:45.230
that people are more able to talk about

00:16:45.230 --> 00:16:46.790
in the ways that others aren't.

00:16:46.790 --> 00:16:49.680
So I've even had members
within academic settings

00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:51.500
from AAPI communities who have talked

00:16:51.500 --> 00:16:53.867
about sometimes those
outs side their community

00:16:53.867 --> 00:16:56.800
who don't know how to enter
into that conversation,

00:16:56.800 --> 00:16:57.640
because they think they're going

00:16:57.640 --> 00:17:00.520
to say something wrong, or
they're worried about missteps.

00:17:00.520 --> 00:17:02.410
Whereas with African
Americans, they don't have

00:17:02.410 --> 00:17:03.570
that same type of hesitation

00:17:03.570 --> 00:17:07.340
because there it's a mutual experience

00:17:07.340 --> 00:17:08.380
and language, so to speak,

00:17:08.380 --> 00:17:09.680
even though it's not the same experience.

00:17:09.680 --> 00:17:12.980
But there are so many
overlapping components

00:17:12.980 --> 00:17:16.170
that people seem to be sharing
across the communities.

00:17:16.170 --> 00:17:18.390
Again, this is a new
thought so it's not quite,

00:17:18.390 --> 00:17:20.220
it's not coming out as
clearly as it is in my head.

00:17:20.220 --> 00:17:21.053
But just something I'm starting

00:17:21.053 --> 00:17:21.886
to know this over time as well.

00:17:21.886 --> 00:17:24.393
I don't know if that's been
your experience as well.

00:17:25.780 --> 00:17:27.810
- I think it's really important for us

00:17:27.810 --> 00:17:32.730
to be able to have these conversations in

00:17:32.730 --> 00:17:36.120
and not be afraid of offending each other

00:17:36.120 --> 00:17:40.450
because that fear just
nips the conversation

00:17:40.450 --> 00:17:41.930
in the bud before it even happens.

00:17:41.930 --> 00:17:44.630
So an example that I often give is

00:17:45.640 --> 00:17:48.060
I interviewed a non-binary
actor by the name

00:17:48.060 --> 00:17:49.860
of Nico Tortorella.

00:17:49.860 --> 00:17:53.140
And I sat down with him and I said,

00:17:53.140 --> 00:17:55.980
I'm really sorry if I misgender you.

00:17:55.980 --> 00:17:58.689
If I use the wrong pronoun,
I don't, you know, da, da.

00:17:58.689 --> 00:18:02.527
And he, they said to
me, see they said to me,

00:18:02.527 --> 00:18:04.610
"Juju, it's totally fine.

00:18:04.610 --> 00:18:06.590
I understand that you're trying,

00:18:06.590 --> 00:18:09.290
and that your intentions are good.

00:18:09.290 --> 00:18:11.810
It really does matter
like where it comes from.

00:18:11.810 --> 00:18:16.530
If somebody misgenders me with
hate then that's not okay.

00:18:16.530 --> 00:18:19.750
But if somebody misgenders me
by accident in conversation,

00:18:19.750 --> 00:18:21.588
I'm not gonna take offense to that."

00:18:21.588 --> 00:18:25.130
And so I think that is the spirit

00:18:25.130 --> 00:18:27.560
in which these conversations
need to happen.

00:18:27.560 --> 00:18:29.095
We have to have that hold grace

00:18:29.095 --> 00:18:31.957
between the person who's speaking

00:18:31.957 --> 00:18:33.890
and the person who's listening.

00:18:33.890 --> 00:18:35.990
I think we talked about Jeremy Lynn

00:18:35.990 --> 00:18:39.500
and one of the most, I think classy ways

00:18:39.500 --> 00:18:44.500
that he discussed a microaggression
or a slur that happened

00:18:46.390 --> 00:18:50.760
to him on court was
somebody on court called him

00:18:50.760 --> 00:18:53.920
the coronavirus or called
him China virus or something.

00:18:53.920 --> 00:18:56.490
And afterwards, everybody the reporters,

00:18:56.490 --> 00:18:58.770
nosy reporters like me were like

00:18:58.770 --> 00:18:59.607
at the press conference saying,

00:18:59.607 --> 00:19:02.050
"So who did it? Who did it? Who was it?"

00:19:02.050 --> 00:19:04.470
And he said, "I have
no interest in shaming

00:19:04.470 --> 00:19:05.890
or blaming anyone.

00:19:05.890 --> 00:19:10.110
But I wanna call out the
behavior because if I don't,

00:19:10.110 --> 00:19:11.790
then it's seen as okay."

00:19:11.790 --> 00:19:15.010
And I thought that is such a great way

00:19:15.010 --> 00:19:16.830
of looking at these conversations

00:19:16.830 --> 00:19:21.262
because we can't learn unless
we have these conversations.

00:19:21.262 --> 00:19:24.200
And yet at the same time we don't wanna be

00:19:25.180 --> 00:19:28.760
just calling balls and
strikes all the time

00:19:28.760 --> 00:19:32.366
and telling people that
what they said was wrong,

00:19:32.366 --> 00:19:35.623
or hurtful or stupid, or worse.

00:19:37.250 --> 00:19:38.270
- That's so well said.

00:19:38.270 --> 00:19:42.110
And even that word, grace that
came up as you were talking

00:19:42.110 --> 00:19:45.440
to that individual, I think
is huge just to have the grace

00:19:45.440 --> 00:19:46.620
to be able to make those mistakes.

00:19:46.620 --> 00:19:48.830
And it seems there's also
a level of trust there

00:19:48.830 --> 00:19:49.663
that needs to be there,

00:19:49.663 --> 00:19:50.650
trust that the people are coming

00:19:50.650 --> 00:19:52.790
from the right place of intentionality.

00:19:52.790 --> 00:19:54.440
And again, just having that place

00:19:55.300 --> 00:19:56.170
to have that learning.

00:19:56.170 --> 00:19:59.075
Because as you said, if
the mistakes don't happen,

00:19:59.075 --> 00:20:00.960
then people won't learn.

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:02.810
And perhaps that's, again,
my neuroscience piece

00:20:02.810 --> 00:20:03.710
that's coming out already,

00:20:03.710 --> 00:20:06.150
but that's just what we learned
from those types of things

00:20:06.150 --> 00:20:08.300
and how that also needs to be applied

00:20:08.300 --> 00:20:10.630
in these situations as well.

00:20:10.630 --> 00:20:11.880
- Absolutely, absolutely.

00:20:13.840 --> 00:20:16.023
- On that note, I also
was curious just about.

00:20:16.023 --> 00:20:18.050
I mean, again, you've
done I keep going back to,

00:20:18.050 --> 00:20:20.260
but just so much that
you've done to really have

00:20:20.260 --> 00:20:22.700
and speak for so many marginalized

00:20:22.700 --> 00:20:24.380
and "voiceless" communities.

00:20:24.380 --> 00:20:25.370
Not that they don't have a voice,

00:20:25.370 --> 00:20:27.370
but not to the level that they need to.

00:20:27.370 --> 00:20:30.400
And I'm curious as you've been
doing this over the years,

00:20:30.400 --> 00:20:32.700
are there specific aspects of your work

00:20:32.700 --> 00:20:34.830
that have been both the most rewarding

00:20:34.830 --> 00:20:37.770
and on the other side
the most challenging?

00:20:37.770 --> 00:20:40.198
So I'll let you start with
whichever one of those one.

00:20:40.198 --> 00:20:41.993
- Oh, so hard.

00:20:42.960 --> 00:20:46.430
The most rewarding one I think was

00:20:48.350 --> 00:20:52.613
at the Atlanta shooting
more than a year ago.

00:20:53.450 --> 00:20:58.450
Because ABC News was the only network

00:20:58.470 --> 00:21:03.140
who did a full prime time
hour following that shooting.

00:21:03.140 --> 00:21:06.682
And I know that, that
wouldn't have happened were it

00:21:06.682 --> 00:21:11.223
for the years and months
of work that I had done,

00:21:13.040 --> 00:21:16.210
pre-interviewing people, doing
interviews with activists,

00:21:16.210 --> 00:21:18.790
having everything sort
of locked and loaded

00:21:18.790 --> 00:21:20.620
so that we could air it within 24 hours.

00:21:20.620 --> 00:21:22.104
I remember flying down there

00:21:22.104 --> 00:21:25.400
and we had booked the son of one victim

00:21:25.400 --> 00:21:26.560
and the daughter of another victim.

00:21:26.560 --> 00:21:29.550
And I was like so angry, 'cuz I was like,

00:21:29.550 --> 00:21:31.880
once again we have to
crash this to the air

00:21:31.880 --> 00:21:33.322
and we don't have time to do it.

00:21:33.322 --> 00:21:35.690
I can't believe we have
to do this in 48 hours.

00:21:35.690 --> 00:21:38.410
And I thought, actually it is in 48 hours.

00:21:38.410 --> 00:21:41.100
I've been working steadily

00:21:41.100 --> 00:21:45.540
towards telling this
particular story for 30 years.

00:21:45.540 --> 00:21:49.180
Because how many mass shoot have I been to

00:21:49.180 --> 00:21:51.830
and delicately had to
speak to a family member?

00:21:51.830 --> 00:21:54.790
How many stories have I
done on human trafficking

00:21:54.790 --> 00:21:59.770
and sex work, and what that
really means in a true sense?

00:21:59.770 --> 00:22:04.040
And the geopolitics of it and the poverty,

00:22:04.040 --> 00:22:07.920
and the deprivation and the
desperation surrounding it.

00:22:07.920 --> 00:22:12.920
And how many of these stories
about AAPI Hate have I done?

00:22:12.940 --> 00:22:16.720
And so all of that felt like
it was a true combination.

00:22:16.720 --> 00:22:19.520
And so that was the story
that I was most proud of.

00:22:19.520 --> 00:22:22.330
And we were able to really get at one

00:22:22.330 --> 00:22:27.330
of the fundamental sort
of areas that is dicey,

00:22:28.730 --> 00:22:31.800
which the hypersexualization
of Asian women.

00:22:31.800 --> 00:22:35.510
And you'll recall there
was a county sheriff

00:22:35.510 --> 00:22:37.790
who came out after speaking to the shooter

00:22:37.790 --> 00:22:39.895
and saying, well, the shooter says,

00:22:39.895 --> 00:22:42.900
he has a sex addiction and
he was trying to get rid

00:22:42.900 --> 00:22:43.800
of his sex addiction.

00:22:43.800 --> 00:22:48.460
So he says, it wasn't about
race, it was about sex.

00:22:48.460 --> 00:22:53.460
At which point Asian American
activists came out very United

00:22:55.080 --> 00:22:59.930
in their opposition to that idea.

00:22:59.930 --> 00:23:04.200
This idea that the shooter
drove 25 plus miles,

00:23:04.200 --> 00:23:09.200
past strip clubs, and porn
shops, and other sex work

00:23:09.480 --> 00:23:12.560
and went at three
different Asian theme spas,

00:23:12.560 --> 00:23:16.910
and conflated any Asian woman
in there as a sex worker.

00:23:16.910 --> 00:23:21.910
And that is racialized at
the very least according

00:23:23.375 --> 00:23:25.600
to so many of the experts that I spoke to.

00:23:25.600 --> 00:23:27.880
So it was a galvanizing moment

00:23:27.880 --> 00:23:29.910
in the Asian American community.

00:23:29.910 --> 00:23:32.750
I interviewed two, as I
said, two victims children's.

00:23:32.750 --> 00:23:35.780
One was a Chinese woman immigrant.

00:23:35.780 --> 00:23:38.050
And she told the story about
her mother who'd come here

00:23:38.050 --> 00:23:41.310
with nothing from a
rural province in China.

00:23:41.310 --> 00:23:46.190
Who had worked after hours
to get a massage license.

00:23:46.190 --> 00:23:49.000
Scraped together pennies,
worked six days a week,

00:23:49.000 --> 00:23:51.874
ate from the rice maker in the back.

00:23:51.874 --> 00:23:53.296
So she could save even more money.

00:23:53.296 --> 00:23:55.470
So she could open her own spa.

00:23:55.470 --> 00:23:57.620
And she had signs in every room that said,

00:23:57.620 --> 00:23:59.530
no sex, no hanky panky.

00:23:59.530 --> 00:24:00.620
And all of her friends said,

00:24:00.620 --> 00:24:03.130
anytime anybody asked
for that kind of stuff,

00:24:03.130 --> 00:24:04.460
they called the police.

00:24:04.460 --> 00:24:09.460
And she was very, very
angry about that conflation.

00:24:10.520 --> 00:24:12.950
Now the flip side of it is
I interviewed a young man

00:24:12.950 --> 00:24:15.610
whose mother worked in a different spa,

00:24:15.610 --> 00:24:17.590
and his name is Randall Park.

00:24:17.590 --> 00:24:19.590
And he said to me, Randy Park.

00:24:19.590 --> 00:24:24.509
And he said, "When I was growing up

00:24:24.509 --> 00:24:27.230
that my mom worked in a beauty salon.

00:24:27.230 --> 00:24:28.873
And, but when I got older,

00:24:30.551 --> 00:24:32.370
I understood that it was shady."

00:24:32.370 --> 00:24:34.990
That was the words that he used.

00:24:34.990 --> 00:24:38.010
And he went and confronted his mother.

00:24:38.010 --> 00:24:41.290
And I asked him what that
conversation was like.

00:24:41.290 --> 00:24:46.130
And he said, would you
tell your son if you worked

00:24:46.130 --> 00:24:49.840
in a spa like that?

00:24:49.840 --> 00:24:52.750
And of course, he's like
about the age of my son

00:24:52.750 --> 00:24:55.270
and I literally stopped short.

00:24:55.270 --> 00:24:58.440
And I knew what I would say,

00:24:58.440 --> 00:25:01.290
but I told him I'm a journalist.

00:25:01.290 --> 00:25:05.160
And so I said to him that's
a really good question.

00:25:05.160 --> 00:25:06.650
And he went on to say,

00:25:06.650 --> 00:25:10.710
I told my mother, let's
not tell my little brother.

00:25:10.710 --> 00:25:12.910
He said, but I want you to be safe.

00:25:12.910 --> 00:25:15.313
I don't like that you lied to me.

00:25:16.603 --> 00:25:17.850
And in closing,

00:25:17.850 --> 00:25:20.528
in the same conversation this
21 year old young man said

00:25:20.528 --> 00:25:23.860
to his mother, I will never shame you

00:25:23.860 --> 00:25:25.430
for the decisions that you made.

00:25:25.430 --> 00:25:28.808
'Cuz I know how hard you
worked to make sure that me

00:25:28.808 --> 00:25:31.953
and my brother as a single mom were okay.

00:25:33.170 --> 00:25:35.120
And I was just blown away by him.

00:25:35.120 --> 00:25:37.860
And he had given up interviews like this,

00:25:37.860 --> 00:25:40.520
and he went on GoFundMe

00:25:40.520 --> 00:25:43.550
and he asked for a little
funeral expenses help.

00:25:43.550 --> 00:25:45.180
Like we live hand to mouth.

00:25:45.180 --> 00:25:48.510
I don't wanna lose this little
apartment to that we have.

00:25:48.510 --> 00:25:50.830
Maybe you could just help me
with some funeral expenses.

00:25:50.830 --> 00:25:55.353
And within 72 hours, he
raised more than $1.7 million.

00:25:56.490 --> 00:25:59.740
And if you go online now and
you GoFund me, Randy Park,

00:25:59.740 --> 00:26:01.373
he's closer to $3 million.

00:26:02.610 --> 00:26:06.300
And I, of course was like,
what in the world is happening?

00:26:06.300 --> 00:26:09.510
And so I scrolled through
all of the GoFund meets

00:26:09.510 --> 00:26:12.150
and it was exactly what I said,

00:26:12.150 --> 00:26:15.670
this idea that it had
this galvanizing impact.

00:26:15.670 --> 00:26:17.490
That so many of the donations,

00:26:17.490 --> 00:26:19.130
they weren't big dollar donations.

00:26:19.130 --> 00:26:22.147
They were like $25, $75, $50.

00:26:22.147 --> 00:26:25.020
And the notes and the
expressions were very similar.

00:26:25.020 --> 00:26:27.910
They were like, we're really
sorry for your loss, Randy,

00:26:27.910 --> 00:26:30.710
we see your pain, Randy,

00:26:30.710 --> 00:26:32.860
my mother sacrificed like your mother.

00:26:32.860 --> 00:26:34.643
And I'm so sorry for your loss.

00:26:35.700 --> 00:26:40.093
On and on and on of
really just being seen.

00:26:41.040 --> 00:26:44.010
And that struggle, that immigrant struggle

00:26:44.010 --> 00:26:46.479
and sacrifice was really visible.

00:26:46.479 --> 00:26:50.570
And so we were really proud
to be able to tell that story.

00:26:50.570 --> 00:26:52.873
And we went back one year later.

00:26:52.873 --> 00:26:55.950
Gave updates on a number of the victim.

00:26:57.720 --> 00:27:01.883
It was an incredibly powerful
amount of storytelling.

00:27:03.860 --> 00:27:06.990
So that was one that I'm most proud of.

00:27:06.990 --> 00:27:10.623
In terms of the ones that
I've found most difficult is,

00:27:13.160 --> 00:27:16.040
I have watched our community,

00:27:16.040 --> 00:27:18.057
the Asian American community be held up

00:27:21.260 --> 00:27:23.550
as the "model minority,"

00:27:23.550 --> 00:27:26.860
which is as you know such mythology,

00:27:26.860 --> 00:27:31.010
it's such out-and-out falsehood because,

00:27:31.010 --> 00:27:34.050
again, I founded the Korean
American Community Foundation

00:27:34.050 --> 00:27:36.880
predicated on the fact that
there's an 18% poverty rate

00:27:36.880 --> 00:27:38.980
for Asian Americans in the tri-state area.

00:27:39.850 --> 00:27:41.268
One in every four Korean Americans

00:27:41.268 --> 00:27:44.610
in Northern New Jersey is uninsured.

00:27:44.610 --> 00:27:48.100
Many are undocumented, many struggle,

00:27:48.100 --> 00:27:49.483
and yet they're invisible.

00:27:50.760 --> 00:27:52.920
These are the Asians that
are not the crazy rich Asians

00:27:52.920 --> 00:27:55.203
that everyone thinks ends up at Yale.

00:27:57.040 --> 00:28:00.160
They're the Asians who bring
you Chinese food dinner

00:28:00.160 --> 00:28:03.720
or polish your nails in the nail salon,

00:28:03.720 --> 00:28:07.010
or give you gas at the gas station,

00:28:07.010 --> 00:28:10.810
or sell you liquor or the
dry cleaning or whatever.

00:28:10.810 --> 00:28:13.560
And these are invisible, their struggle

00:28:13.560 --> 00:28:15.550
and their pain is often in invisible.

00:28:15.550 --> 00:28:19.270
And I think for me, it's been difficult

00:28:19.270 --> 00:28:20.747
to watch our community.

00:28:20.747 --> 00:28:23.272
It's hard because it's like,
oh, it's a good stereotype.

00:28:23.272 --> 00:28:24.720
Why would you not like that?

00:28:24.720 --> 00:28:27.830
It's like, well, not only
does it put undue pressure

00:28:27.830 --> 00:28:29.315
on those in the community.

00:28:29.315 --> 00:28:32.520
I'm sure you see it among
your Asian American students,

00:28:32.520 --> 00:28:36.686
but it also, for those who
don't fit that stereotype,

00:28:36.686 --> 00:28:38.740
it's a really negative.

00:28:38.740 --> 00:28:40.450
And then that stereotype is used

00:28:40.450 --> 00:28:44.010
to put our community against
other communities of color.

00:28:44.010 --> 00:28:49.010
And so that is another sort of
pain point for our community.

00:28:52.100 --> 00:28:55.000
It's led to so much misunderstanding

00:28:55.000 --> 00:29:00.000
and at a time when we should be working

00:29:00.360 --> 00:29:03.633
in solidarity on mutual.

00:29:06.540 --> 00:29:10.143
We should all band together to
fight hate period, full stop.

00:29:11.060 --> 00:29:15.010
White allies, Hispanic allies,
African American allies.

00:29:15.010 --> 00:29:16.470
We should all be allied together.

00:29:16.470 --> 00:29:19.080
And so to me it's been painful

00:29:19.080 --> 00:29:23.083
to watch some of those stories emerge.

00:29:24.010 --> 00:29:24.843
- Really well said.

00:29:24.843 --> 00:29:26.064
I mean, so many things that resonate

00:29:26.064 --> 00:29:28.620
and so much to unpack as well.

00:29:28.620 --> 00:29:30.510
One thing that I want
to actually go back to

00:29:30.510 --> 00:29:32.913
in the first story that
you were sharing about

00:29:32.913 --> 00:29:35.160
that just being a galvanizing moment.

00:29:35.160 --> 00:29:37.190
Because again having had a lot of

00:29:37.190 --> 00:29:40.015
I've had Korean American
roommates in college

00:29:40.015 --> 00:29:41.090
and a lot of other Asian American friends,

00:29:41.090 --> 00:29:42.120
and just thinking about things

00:29:42.120 --> 00:29:44.650
that have been happening over the years

00:29:44.650 --> 00:29:47.310
that have not come to
the surface, so to speak.

00:29:47.310 --> 00:29:48.340
I mean, even when you were talking

00:29:48.340 --> 00:29:50.250
about the tragedy in Atlanta,

00:29:50.250 --> 00:29:54.210
how your network was the only
network to do what you did.

00:29:54.210 --> 00:29:57.520
And so I'm wondering how
that has been affecting

00:29:57.520 --> 00:30:00.484
the Asian American community
already up till this point

00:30:00.484 --> 00:30:03.460
and what you see as a shift.

00:30:03.460 --> 00:30:04.450
'Cause even as you talked about,

00:30:04.450 --> 00:30:06.310
you have been preparing
that story for years,

00:30:06.310 --> 00:30:07.930
not just because of the
work you've been doing

00:30:07.930 --> 00:30:09.910
around anti-Asian hate,

00:30:09.910 --> 00:30:11.670
but because of the work you've been doing

00:30:11.670 --> 00:30:15.770
around marginalization and
discrimination in general.

00:30:15.770 --> 00:30:17.265
But again, it seems like
so much of it has been,

00:30:17.265 --> 00:30:19.113
as you said, under the surface.

00:30:20.590 --> 00:30:21.860
How do you see things shifting,

00:30:21.860 --> 00:30:24.548
are there things that
are moving in the way

00:30:24.548 --> 00:30:26.048
that they need to?

00:30:26.890 --> 00:30:30.230
- I mean, I see a lot of
change in the last two years.

00:30:30.230 --> 00:30:33.110
I think I started seeing it

00:30:34.547 --> 00:30:37.610
in the Black Lives Matter protests.

00:30:37.610 --> 00:30:38.720
'Cause as a journalist,

00:30:38.720 --> 00:30:43.720
I was out covering those
protests face to police shield

00:30:44.312 --> 00:30:48.110
in the middle of the mask protests.

00:30:48.110 --> 00:30:49.770
But when I looked around,

00:30:49.770 --> 00:30:53.060
I had covered many protests
in my 30 year career,

00:30:53.060 --> 00:30:55.700
but these protestors
looked more like America.

00:30:55.700 --> 00:31:00.543
They were parents and children
protesting, grandparents,

00:31:01.480 --> 00:31:02.580
the black community,

00:31:02.580 --> 00:31:04.800
obviously the Latino community.

00:31:04.800 --> 00:31:07.740
I had never seen so many
Asian Americans protesting

00:31:09.360 --> 00:31:10.570
for Black Lives Matter.

00:31:10.570 --> 00:31:13.994
And I thought that was
such like an awakening.

00:31:13.994 --> 00:31:18.350
And in many ways tool gathering,

00:31:18.350 --> 00:31:20.440
if you know what I'm saying.

00:31:20.440 --> 00:31:25.440
And it really, to me
opened people's hearts

00:31:27.460 --> 00:31:28.860
and minds, I think.

00:31:28.860 --> 00:31:32.883
And then when the AAPPI Hate
stuff started happening,

00:31:34.040 --> 00:31:38.680
I really felt like I saw a maturation.

00:31:38.680 --> 00:31:41.090
So the points of light to
me are the young activists

00:31:41.090 --> 00:31:43.300
who were going to the olders

00:31:43.300 --> 00:31:46.060
and saying, "You know what, mom, dad,

00:31:46.060 --> 00:31:48.350
grandpa you're vulnerable.

00:31:48.350 --> 00:31:51.070
And you can't stick
with the cultural norms

00:31:51.070 --> 00:31:52.170
of keep your head down.

00:31:52.170 --> 00:31:54.550
Don't say anything, don't raise ruckus.

00:31:54.550 --> 00:31:56.580
You need to go and report this.

00:31:56.580 --> 00:31:58.357
You need to go and make
your rights matter."

00:31:58.357 --> 00:32:01.890
And I heard somebody, a
young comedian on TikTok.

00:32:01.890 --> 00:32:03.897
My kids hate it when I mention TikTok,

00:32:03.897 --> 00:32:06.147
but he said he was a, I
think he was a South Asian.

00:32:06.147 --> 00:32:10.283
And he said, "My family
called it, the Uncle Sam tax.

00:32:11.450 --> 00:32:15.180
They were willing to be treated
like second class citizens

00:32:15.180 --> 00:32:18.010
because of the bounty
that this country offers.

00:32:18.010 --> 00:32:19.530
And he said, but my generation,

00:32:19.530 --> 00:32:21.073
we're not willing to do that.

00:32:22.235 --> 00:32:26.900
We all took his AP history
and political science,

00:32:26.900 --> 00:32:28.520
and we have the audacity to believe

00:32:28.520 --> 00:32:30.480
that we deserve equal
protection under the law,

00:32:30.480 --> 00:32:32.240
that we deserve equal rights,

00:32:32.240 --> 00:32:35.210
that we therefore are Americans.

00:32:35.210 --> 00:32:39.400
And it was such a powerful
kind of rallying cry

00:32:39.400 --> 00:32:42.313
for a comedian, really lovely.

00:32:43.220 --> 00:32:44.880
But I think that's kind of what I'm seeing

00:32:44.880 --> 00:32:45.720
in the young generation.

00:32:45.720 --> 00:32:50.720
I also think that I'm seeing
Jonathan Greenblatt of the ADL,

00:32:51.700 --> 00:32:54.260
the anti-defamation like
runs the Center for Hate.

00:32:54.260 --> 00:32:57.750
And he saw a lot of the
online hate spiking.

00:32:57.750 --> 00:33:00.120
And he went to the
Asian American community

00:33:00.120 --> 00:33:01.860
and warned them.

00:33:01.860 --> 00:33:06.520
And that is how the Asian
American Foundation was created.

00:33:07.410 --> 00:33:09.417
And it was launched by very

00:33:11.210 --> 00:33:14.860
sort of powerful philanthropists,

00:33:14.860 --> 00:33:17.680
including like Joe Tsai, Joe Bay,

00:33:17.680 --> 00:33:19.670
Joe Tsai who owns the Nets.

00:33:19.670 --> 00:33:22.788
Joe Bay, all these Jerry Yang,

00:33:22.788 --> 00:33:26.460
Like all these very high profile people

00:33:26.460 --> 00:33:30.400
who leveraged their considerable power

00:33:30.400 --> 00:33:33.090
and wealth and founded the
Asian American Foundation

00:33:33.090 --> 00:33:34.500
to help.

00:33:34.500 --> 00:33:37.763
Again, this was the galvanizing
moment that happened.

00:33:38.775 --> 00:33:42.678
And then I see like grassroots
community organizers,

00:33:42.678 --> 00:33:45.980
people who are in Chinatown
who are like young kids,

00:33:45.980 --> 00:33:48.130
who are like doing stuff.

00:33:48.130 --> 00:33:53.130
And in Georgia, there was
a big get out the vote,

00:33:53.175 --> 00:33:58.175
sort of enrollment and
were told by activists

00:33:58.470 --> 00:34:02.456
that, that was the crucial margin

00:34:02.456 --> 00:34:05.650
for the two Senate races in Georgia.

00:34:05.650 --> 00:34:10.650
And I've seen Asian American organizations

00:34:11.220 --> 00:34:14.591
over the years do like, oh,
let's ask for bilingual ballots,

00:34:14.591 --> 00:34:16.830
let's look for more ballot access.

00:34:16.830 --> 00:34:18.280
Let's do this kind of stuff

00:34:18.280 --> 00:34:20.300
that literally the work of democracy.

00:34:20.300 --> 00:34:24.353
And I think that, that has
accelerated because of this.

00:34:26.350 --> 00:34:29.120
There are points of light, so to speak.

00:34:29.120 --> 00:34:30.420
- That's really encouraging to hear.

00:34:30.420 --> 00:34:31.253
I mean, just in the way

00:34:31.253 --> 00:34:33.690
that some of those organizations
have come to light,

00:34:33.690 --> 00:34:35.006
and the way that people have been invested

00:34:35.006 --> 00:34:38.370
in that work as well,
and that's so important.

00:34:38.370 --> 00:34:40.260
And of course, I'm always, again thinking

00:34:40.260 --> 00:34:41.610
about the neuroscience and mental health.

00:34:41.610 --> 00:34:44.850
I'm always just thinking
about just the tax as it were

00:34:44.850 --> 00:34:47.580
of all these things on the
Asian American community.

00:34:47.580 --> 00:34:49.170
And so I'm wondering if you'd be wanting

00:34:49.170 --> 00:34:51.185
to share a little bit first
about just your perceptions

00:34:51.185 --> 00:34:54.880
about mental health
conversations in AAPI communities

00:34:54.880 --> 00:34:57.900
and how that intersects in a way

00:34:57.900 --> 00:35:00.260
with all that's happened
around anti-Asian aid,

00:35:00.260 --> 00:35:02.360
how that can also influence that,

00:35:02.360 --> 00:35:04.710
and just if things are moving
in terms of conversation,

00:35:04.710 --> 00:35:05.543
we had a little bit

00:35:05.543 --> 00:35:08.167
of that conversation a couple
episodes ago with Jeremy Lynn,

00:35:08.167 --> 00:35:09.770
as you mentioned, Esther Chiu,

00:35:09.770 --> 00:35:13.203
but just curious to hear your
perspective on that as well.

00:35:14.110 --> 00:35:18.372
- Well, I think there are
many different directions

00:35:18.372 --> 00:35:21.130
to turn the telescope.

00:35:21.130 --> 00:35:24.820
I mean, there's the I idea that
Asian American young people

00:35:24.820 --> 00:35:27.810
are often suffering
higher rates of anxiety

00:35:27.810 --> 00:35:30.310
and stress and depression,

00:35:30.310 --> 00:35:34.100
because I think of the sort of pressure

00:35:34.100 --> 00:35:35.699
of living up to the model minority myth.

00:35:35.699 --> 00:35:37.550
Often when your parents,

00:35:37.550 --> 00:35:41.120
when your parents have
sacrificed everything for you,

00:35:41.120 --> 00:35:43.600
that's a lot of pressure, you
don't wanna screw that up.

00:35:43.600 --> 00:35:46.370
And you feel very burdened by that.

00:35:46.370 --> 00:35:50.330
At the same time, the Asian
American community is very,

00:35:50.330 --> 00:35:51.580
I mean, I hate to speak broadly

00:35:51.580 --> 00:35:54.393
'cuz we know the Asian American
community represents dozens

00:35:54.393 --> 00:35:57.410
of countries and even more are cultures,

00:35:57.410 --> 00:36:00.686
and languages and faith,
traditions and all of that.

00:36:00.686 --> 00:36:05.150
But if I were to say
culturally Asian Americans,

00:36:05.150 --> 00:36:10.150
there's a very big stigma
surrounding mental health.

00:36:10.244 --> 00:36:12.990
There isn't a lot of open discussion.

00:36:12.990 --> 00:36:16.050
There isn't a lot of
therapy, treatment options.

00:36:16.050 --> 00:36:18.420
There isn't a lot in that space.

00:36:18.420 --> 00:36:22.000
And then thirdly, I would
say, when you drill down

00:36:22.000 --> 00:36:26.070
into these attacks, many
of them involve a component

00:36:26.070 --> 00:36:30.610
of a mental health break on
the part of the perpetrators.

00:36:30.610 --> 00:36:32.790
And we've seen that time and time again,

00:36:32.790 --> 00:36:35.779
whether it's somebody who
walks into a black church

00:36:35.779 --> 00:36:37.580
and kills parishioners.

00:36:37.580 --> 00:36:40.630
Whether it's somebody who
walks into a Jewish synagogue

00:36:40.630 --> 00:36:42.460
and kills parishioners,

00:36:42.460 --> 00:36:45.050
or somebody who walks into
three Asian theme spas

00:36:45.050 --> 00:36:48.563
and kills Asian Americans and bystanders.

00:36:50.774 --> 00:36:53.250
This is disordered behavior.

00:36:53.250 --> 00:36:55.660
This is mental illness.

00:36:55.660 --> 00:37:00.660
And yet a lot of this
is reframed depending

00:37:02.420 --> 00:37:04.197
on the circumstances.

00:37:04.197 --> 00:37:06.440
And I think that ultimately

00:37:06.440 --> 00:37:09.222
that is why dog whistles are so dangerous.

00:37:09.222 --> 00:37:11.399
It's not because rational
people are gonna go out

00:37:11.399 --> 00:37:15.073
and attack Asian people
because of the pandemic,

00:37:16.160 --> 00:37:18.970
it's because irrational people may.

00:37:18.970 --> 00:37:22.440
And there's a lot of
intersection of poverty

00:37:22.440 --> 00:37:27.430
and homelessness, and
mental health crises.

00:37:27.430 --> 00:37:32.430
And so the solutions are very complicated

00:37:34.270 --> 00:37:39.270
and complex, and multi-layered,
and they are not.

00:37:39.600 --> 00:37:42.060
Nothing's quick, there's no quick answers.

00:37:42.060 --> 00:37:43.945
And so people who are like, oh, let's...

00:37:43.945 --> 00:37:47.860
The other thing that's tricky
is that some of this flies

00:37:47.860 --> 00:37:51.890
into the face of the defund debate.

00:37:51.890 --> 00:37:54.890
Because there are those who argue,

00:37:54.890 --> 00:37:56.290
well, we need more policing.

00:37:56.290 --> 00:38:00.510
We need stronger police
presence, police force.

00:38:00.510 --> 00:38:02.690
And then there are others who say,

00:38:02.690 --> 00:38:05.969
no, this is exactly why we
need more mental health.

00:38:05.969 --> 00:38:08.640
We need more culturally
competent police officers

00:38:08.640 --> 00:38:11.500
who can speak the
different Asian languages,

00:38:11.500 --> 00:38:12.402
and be culturally competent.

00:38:12.402 --> 00:38:14.170
So that if somebody is victimized,

00:38:14.170 --> 00:38:15.670
they're not afraid to go to the police

00:38:15.670 --> 00:38:18.187
because in the old country,
you didn't go to the police.

00:38:18.187 --> 00:38:19.443
That kind of stuff.

00:38:20.370 --> 00:38:23.610
How do you get a hate crimes
charged when the witnesses

00:38:23.610 --> 00:38:25.820
or the victim don't speak the language,

00:38:25.820 --> 00:38:28.300
or not well enough to come up?

00:38:28.300 --> 00:38:33.300
So there are so many complexities
that need to be examined.

00:38:33.600 --> 00:38:38.600
Unfortunately, we don't have
a very patient political body.

00:38:40.324 --> 00:38:43.450
And so these very nuanced solutions

00:38:43.450 --> 00:38:47.580
and complicated solutions
are hard to get at,

00:38:47.580 --> 00:38:48.773
to say the very least.

00:38:49.840 --> 00:38:54.270
But I do think that it's from
a mental health perspective,

00:38:54.270 --> 00:38:56.710
from neuroscience perspective,

00:38:56.710 --> 00:39:00.030
so much of what we've endured

00:39:00.030 --> 00:39:05.030
as a society during the
pandemic is flaring out.

00:39:05.100 --> 00:39:09.620
I'm sure you would agree in
so many different spheres.

00:39:09.620 --> 00:39:14.340
And so you're seeing this
giant uptick in gun violence,

00:39:14.340 --> 00:39:16.920
you're seeing uptick in violent crime.

00:39:16.920 --> 00:39:19.340
You're seeing an uptick in self harm.

00:39:19.340 --> 00:39:20.320
You're seeing an uptick

00:39:20.320 --> 00:39:24.630
in all of these mental health arenas

00:39:24.630 --> 00:39:27.166
that again are often reframed.

00:39:27.166 --> 00:39:30.003
And so hate crimes are sadly among them.

00:39:31.257 --> 00:39:32.230
- And there's so many,

00:39:32.230 --> 00:39:33.419
as you mentioned so
many things that we have

00:39:33.419 --> 00:39:36.190
to pay attention to on
so many different scales.

00:39:36.190 --> 00:39:38.860
I mean, even thinking
going back to childhood,

00:39:38.860 --> 00:39:41.350
thinking about what needs
to happen in the schools,

00:39:41.350 --> 00:39:44.210
because as students are
going through everything,

00:39:44.210 --> 00:39:45.680
as parents are going through all that,

00:39:45.680 --> 00:39:47.430
as those combined together,

00:39:47.430 --> 00:39:48.910
all the anxieties uncertainties

00:39:48.910 --> 00:39:51.310
that people are navigating simultaneously

00:39:51.310 --> 00:39:53.760
in certain situations as
you add on top of that,

00:39:53.760 --> 00:39:54.870
all the things like the stories

00:39:54.870 --> 00:39:56.581
that you've been covering for
years are just gonna become

00:39:56.581 --> 00:39:58.963
that much more exacerbated.

00:39:59.807 --> 00:40:02.280
I think you're exactly
right in that component.

00:40:02.280 --> 00:40:04.760
And yet at the same time, even
as you mentioned early on,

00:40:04.760 --> 00:40:08.110
you still see these aspects
and these reasons for hope.

00:40:08.110 --> 00:40:10.760
So even in the midst of that seems

00:40:10.760 --> 00:40:12.060
that there's still something

00:40:12.060 --> 00:40:14.400
that keeps you going on and
talking about these stories,

00:40:14.400 --> 00:40:16.750
but then seeing the hope
that people are have having

00:40:16.750 --> 00:40:19.397
to move forward as things have
been galvanized in a sense.

00:40:19.397 --> 00:40:21.453
And I'm just curious what gives you hope

00:40:21.453 --> 00:40:22.820
on a day to day basis?

00:40:22.820 --> 00:40:25.270
'Cause I'm thinking
about being in your shoes

00:40:25.270 --> 00:40:28.030
and just walking into certain situations,

00:40:28.030 --> 00:40:30.230
how heavy that must be.

00:40:30.230 --> 00:40:32.890
And how do you enter into that space

00:40:32.890 --> 00:40:34.300
and how do you exit out that space

00:40:34.300 --> 00:40:37.087
to still have hope for
the future going forward?

00:40:37.087 --> 00:40:38.733
- It's a great question.

00:40:40.810 --> 00:40:42.220
I've tried meditation.

00:40:42.220 --> 00:40:44.369
I'm a lousy meditator.

00:40:44.369 --> 00:40:45.633
- [Addy] That's good to know.

00:40:45.633 --> 00:40:49.913
- Thank you. I try, I try.

00:40:49.913 --> 00:40:52.980
There's a form of loving
kindness meditation

00:40:52.980 --> 00:40:55.340
where you're supposed to meditate

00:40:55.340 --> 00:40:57.510
and spread, loving kindness, whatever.

00:40:57.510 --> 00:41:01.026
But at you start by sending
loving kindness to yourself.

00:41:01.026 --> 00:41:04.260
And I think that, that
we be beat ourselves up.

00:41:04.260 --> 00:41:08.040
We're so hard on ourselves.
We're so mean to ourselves.

00:41:08.040 --> 00:41:13.040
I think that is a big part of
how I try to lift myself up.

00:41:13.775 --> 00:41:17.640
I wanna share a story about
one victim I interviewed,

00:41:17.640 --> 00:41:20.940
her name is Velma Carey,
Filipino American.

00:41:20.940 --> 00:41:22.980
She was beaten up in front
of a Midtown high rise.

00:41:22.980 --> 00:41:25.030
And the highlight of her beating was

00:41:25.030 --> 00:41:29.410
that the lobby folks were
seen closing the door

00:41:29.410 --> 00:41:31.210
as this beating was happening.

00:41:31.210 --> 00:41:34.090
And later the building
said, "You only saw a part

00:41:34.090 --> 00:41:35.270
of the clip you didn't see all of it."

00:41:35.270 --> 00:41:38.963
But the bottom line was, it
created a lot of awareness

00:41:41.270 --> 00:41:42.550
on her particular attack.

00:41:42.550 --> 00:41:44.360
And so it took us weeks,

00:41:44.360 --> 00:41:46.370
but we finally convinced her daughter

00:41:46.370 --> 00:41:47.930
and her to sit down for an interview,

00:41:47.930 --> 00:41:50.640
and they did such powerful stuff.

00:41:50.640 --> 00:41:52.443
So they had too had done a GoFundMe.

00:41:52.443 --> 00:41:55.399
They raised a couple $100,000
which was significant.

00:41:55.399 --> 00:41:59.080
And the daughter took that money

00:41:59.080 --> 00:42:03.660
and created a popup museum with stories.

00:42:05.270 --> 00:42:08.130
So what happened was during
the attack, the attacker,

00:42:08.130 --> 00:42:11.010
just again, to bring some
humanity to the attacker,

00:42:11.010 --> 00:42:13.490
he was having a mental health break.

00:42:13.490 --> 00:42:16.733
He was just let out of prison
for killing his own mother.

00:42:17.635 --> 00:42:21.370
There was a tremendous
amount of trauma there

00:42:21.370 --> 00:42:25.617
and he beat up this random woman and said,

00:42:25.617 --> 00:42:30.617
"You Asian B word, you don't belong here."

00:42:31.030 --> 00:42:32.660
And so the daughter took these stories

00:42:32.660 --> 00:42:35.260
of belonging from the AAPI community

00:42:35.260 --> 00:42:38.830
and created exhibits using these words,

00:42:38.830 --> 00:42:42.313
and entitled it AAPI Belong.

00:42:43.950 --> 00:42:46.520
And so then I'm sitting down
with Velma and her daughter,

00:42:46.520 --> 00:42:49.040
and Velma says, I said,
"So, did you ever make it

00:42:49.040 --> 00:42:50.170
to St Patrick's Cathedral?

00:42:50.170 --> 00:42:52.550
'Cuz I heard that was where
you were headed that day."

00:42:52.550 --> 00:42:55.700
And she said, "Yes I did. I
went for my 60th birthday."

00:42:55.700 --> 00:42:57.070
I said, "What did you pray for?"

00:42:57.070 --> 00:42:59.420
And she just had this
big smile on her face.

00:42:59.420 --> 00:43:02.020
And she said, "Prayers of gratitude."

00:43:02.020 --> 00:43:07.020
She said, "We had so much
loving outpouring of food.

00:43:07.980 --> 00:43:09.760
People were sending food and gifts

00:43:09.760 --> 00:43:12.660
and notes of encouragement
and money and the GoFundMe.

00:43:12.660 --> 00:43:13.560
And I couldn't believe like

00:43:13.560 --> 00:43:16.460
how much people she showed
their compassion to me."

00:43:16.460 --> 00:43:17.790
And then she looked at her daughter

00:43:17.790 --> 00:43:19.137
and she looked at me and she said,

00:43:19.137 --> 00:43:21.997
"And then I prayed for my attacker."

00:43:23.130 --> 00:43:26.080
And literally the camera on me
at the moment was like this.

00:43:27.580 --> 00:43:30.230
And I said, "Wow, why,
why did you do that?"

00:43:30.230 --> 00:43:34.700
And she said, "Because whatever
was going on in his mind,

00:43:34.700 --> 00:43:36.710
I wanted to give him grace."

00:43:36.710 --> 00:43:39.520
That was literally the
words that she used.

00:43:39.520 --> 00:43:42.053
And I sat there for a
second and I said to her,

00:43:43.300 --> 00:43:45.300
again, I've been doing this for 30 years

00:43:45.300 --> 00:43:47.187
and I've talked to a lot of
people about a lot of things.

00:43:47.187 --> 00:43:50.122
And I said, but I know
enough whether you look at it

00:43:50.122 --> 00:43:53.730
from the neuroscience perspective

00:43:53.730 --> 00:43:57.733
or from the spiritual
perspective or from the

00:44:00.180 --> 00:44:01.950
philosophical perspective,

00:44:01.950 --> 00:44:06.950
that like that act of
forgiving someone does as much,

00:44:07.370 --> 00:44:11.670
if not more to empower the
person who's doing the forgiving

00:44:11.670 --> 00:44:16.000
then it does bestow on the
person who's being forgiven.

00:44:16.000 --> 00:44:18.567
And so in that moment I said to her,

00:44:18.567 --> 00:44:20.500
"You know what's really crazy, Velma

00:44:20.500 --> 00:44:25.422
is that you're literally
fighting hate with love."

00:44:25.422 --> 00:44:28.610
And it was just such a
powerful moment for me.

00:44:28.610 --> 00:44:31.050
And I thought it was
so obviously so genuine

00:44:31.050 --> 00:44:34.817
from her sort of Catholic perspective.

00:44:34.817 --> 00:44:36.660
And so that's the kind of stuff

00:44:36.660 --> 00:44:40.050
that gives me hope that
like makes me think

00:44:40.050 --> 00:44:45.050
that we can turn this a hateful
act into an piece of art.

00:44:47.990 --> 00:44:50.847
AAPI Belong to turn victimization

00:44:50.847 --> 00:44:54.040
into something more
powerful and meaningful.

00:44:54.040 --> 00:44:59.040
And then to take this incident
and surround it with meaning.

00:45:00.620 --> 00:45:02.600
- Wow. That's so beautiful.

00:45:02.600 --> 00:45:03.433
So well said.

00:45:03.433 --> 00:45:06.579
- Crazy right? I know she was so powerful.

00:45:06.579 --> 00:45:09.210
- And you also, you took
the words out of my mouth

00:45:09.210 --> 00:45:11.600
in terms of that holistic
approach to thing as well.

00:45:11.600 --> 00:45:13.100
'Cause I mean, that's so
much of what we talk about

00:45:13.100 --> 00:45:14.280
on this podcast,

00:45:14.280 --> 00:45:16.650
thinking about all those
different perspectives,

00:45:16.650 --> 00:45:18.670
whether like you said, neuroscience,

00:45:18.670 --> 00:45:19.890
the spiritual component,

00:45:19.890 --> 00:45:21.230
the psychological component

00:45:21.230 --> 00:45:23.560
and how all these things intersect.

00:45:23.560 --> 00:45:26.260
And so I think that's just
a beautiful encapsulation

00:45:26.260 --> 00:45:27.093
of all that.

00:45:27.093 --> 00:45:28.708
And it's so important.
I mean, I'm trying not

00:45:28.708 --> 00:45:30.220
to pull in all the neuroscience,

00:45:30.220 --> 00:45:32.430
but what that does, all those
components what those do

00:45:32.430 --> 00:45:35.020
to our brains and how that helps us

00:45:35.020 --> 00:45:36.251
to come up with different
coping strategies

00:45:36.251 --> 00:45:38.270
and to move through.

00:45:38.270 --> 00:45:39.580
Even in our last conversation,

00:45:39.580 --> 00:45:41.991
we had a conversation with
a psychiatrist who also

00:45:41.991 --> 00:45:44.990
is a man of faith and talked
about all the evidence

00:45:44.990 --> 00:45:47.990
for the spiritual practices
when they are tied together

00:45:47.990 --> 00:45:49.730
with different
psychological interventions,

00:45:49.730 --> 00:45:51.390
and how powerful that can be.

00:45:51.390 --> 00:45:54.280
And it seems like even
that's not your profession,

00:45:54.280 --> 00:45:56.517
how you've seen that at work
in so many different ways.

00:45:56.517 --> 00:45:58.810
That's really, really powerful

00:45:58.810 --> 00:46:01.330
and really encouraging
to hear about as well.

00:46:01.330 --> 00:46:02.551
The one piece I was also gonna ask

00:46:02.551 --> 00:46:05.240
about as we wrap up is just your,

00:46:05.240 --> 00:46:06.997
I know this may feel a little bit,

00:46:08.030 --> 00:46:09.070
like I'm pulling it back in,

00:46:09.070 --> 00:46:11.880
but just thinking about
your faith walk as well

00:46:11.880 --> 00:46:13.940
as you are someone who grew up

00:46:13.940 --> 00:46:15.590
in a Korean American household,

00:46:15.590 --> 00:46:18.387
but then also converted
to Judaism at one point.

00:46:18.387 --> 00:46:20.100
And so, as you know as
we brought that part

00:46:20.100 --> 00:46:23.400
into the conversation curious
how that journey came about

00:46:23.400 --> 00:46:25.940
and how that has also
impacted your outlook

00:46:25.940 --> 00:46:27.340
on all the work that you do?

00:46:29.160 --> 00:46:30.873
- I was the reluctant Jew.

00:46:32.370 --> 00:46:33.980
I've actually never you used that phrase,

00:46:33.980 --> 00:46:35.960
but I think it's true.

00:46:35.960 --> 00:46:40.220
I married a nice Jewish
guy named Neil Shapiro

00:46:40.220 --> 00:46:42.990
and I told him when we were dating

00:46:42.990 --> 00:46:46.510
and after we got married that
I was never gonna convert.

00:46:46.510 --> 00:46:49.100
So like end of topic, move on.

00:46:49.100 --> 00:46:51.820
I was raised as this you know I felt

00:46:51.820 --> 00:46:53.690
I was very proud secular humanist.

00:46:53.690 --> 00:46:57.060
And I was maybe a little bit
of a situational emphasis,

00:46:57.060 --> 00:46:57.893
but whatever.

00:46:59.323 --> 00:47:03.050
But after like a dozen years together

00:47:03.050 --> 00:47:06.176
after having some kids,
or one kid at the time,

00:47:06.176 --> 00:47:07.640
we ended up having three boys.

00:47:07.640 --> 00:47:08.890
But at the time one,

00:47:08.890 --> 00:47:13.890
I realized I really drew a lot
of energy from the rituals,

00:47:23.010 --> 00:47:27.320
from the philosophy, from the thinking,

00:47:27.320 --> 00:47:29.430
from the thoughtfulness of Judaism.

00:47:29.430 --> 00:47:32.040
And so one day I literally said

00:47:32.040 --> 00:47:33.550
to my husband over breakfast,

00:47:33.550 --> 00:47:35.100
like, I think I'm gonna convert.

00:47:35.100 --> 00:47:37.573
And he literally did
one of the, like what?

00:47:39.840 --> 00:47:42.340
And what was great is
we spent a year going

00:47:42.340 --> 00:47:45.293
to like introduction to
Judaism classes together.

00:47:47.263 --> 00:47:50.100
We talked about things that
we've never talked about

00:47:50.100 --> 00:47:51.410
in the dozen years we've been together.

00:47:51.410 --> 00:47:53.760
And that was what was so enriching

00:47:53.760 --> 00:47:56.790
and in an interesting
way, it changed his view

00:47:56.790 --> 00:47:57.810
of his own Judaism.

00:47:57.810 --> 00:48:01.830
Because the rabbi went
from the guy up on the Bema

00:48:01.830 --> 00:48:05.790
to the guy sitting next to us
chatting about the Yankees.

00:48:05.790 --> 00:48:08.300
And so that changed his perspective too.

00:48:08.300 --> 00:48:11.650
And for me we've been
part of the lay leadership

00:48:11.650 --> 00:48:12.490
in the congregation.

00:48:12.490 --> 00:48:14.430
Our kids are very identified,

00:48:14.430 --> 00:48:17.213
very strongly as both
Jews and Korean Americans.

00:48:18.430 --> 00:48:20.430
The joke in our house is
that they're 50% Korean

00:48:20.430 --> 00:48:21.793
and 100% Jewish.

00:48:24.990 --> 00:48:28.873
And so it's been an
incredible source of strength,

00:48:32.540 --> 00:48:35.810
and family, and love like,

00:48:35.810 --> 00:48:39.283
we just last week, I hosted two seders.

00:48:40.460 --> 00:48:42.130
I make a pretty good latka

00:48:42.130 --> 00:48:44.781
and you're welcome to come next time.

00:48:44.781 --> 00:48:46.570
And my meatball soup,

00:48:46.570 --> 00:48:49.030
even though the meatballs are from a box

00:48:49.030 --> 00:48:49.972
are still pretty good.

00:48:49.972 --> 00:48:51.222
- [Addy] That still
works. That still works.

00:48:51.222 --> 00:48:52.400
- It still works.

00:48:52.400 --> 00:48:54.173
I cut some corners here and there.

00:48:55.780 --> 00:48:57.650
It's really part of our family tradition.

00:48:57.650 --> 00:49:01.110
bUt I also make dumplings from
scratch and rice cake soup

00:49:01.110 --> 00:49:03.150
for new year's day.

00:49:03.150 --> 00:49:05.920
And so I feel like my faith is something

00:49:05.920 --> 00:49:10.820
that it literally was an act of faith

00:49:11.710 --> 00:49:16.433
to decide that I wanted to be a Jew.

00:49:18.190 --> 00:49:20.503
And live as one.

00:49:22.410 --> 00:49:23.960
- Has that shaped your perspective

00:49:23.960 --> 00:49:26.770
in your work after that conversion?

00:49:26.770 --> 00:49:27.610
- Think so.

00:49:27.610 --> 00:49:30.900
Absolutely, I mean it kind of dovetails

00:49:30.900 --> 00:49:33.900
because at the seder every year,

00:49:33.900 --> 00:49:37.940
we talk about treating strangers,

00:49:37.940 --> 00:49:39.880
remembering to be kind to strangers

00:49:39.880 --> 00:49:42.180
because you are one
strangers in a strange land.

00:49:42.180 --> 00:49:45.430
To understand that oppression
everywhere is anywhere

00:49:45.430 --> 00:49:49.700
is something that Jews stand up against.

00:49:49.700 --> 00:49:52.250
And it's so interesting
that it into weaving,

00:49:52.250 --> 00:49:54.913
like right now, I told you
the story about the ADL

00:49:54.913 --> 00:49:56.700
and the AAPI community.

00:49:56.700 --> 00:50:00.320
And I talk often about how
Frederick Douglas spoke out

00:50:00.320 --> 00:50:04.400
against the Chinese Exclusion
Act back at the time

00:50:04.400 --> 00:50:05.820
in the 1880s.

00:50:05.820 --> 00:50:10.303
And how the Jews worked
in the civil rights sphere

00:50:11.379 --> 00:50:16.379
and in that historical brotherhood really.

00:50:16.630 --> 00:50:21.630
And that is something that I
wish was a better understood

00:50:23.701 --> 00:50:27.255
than the things that I see right now,

00:50:27.255 --> 00:50:31.544
especially online where hate gets clicks.

00:50:31.544 --> 00:50:36.179
And there's a lot of anti this, anti that.

00:50:36.179 --> 00:50:39.630
You are stepping out you're
this, and there's this,

00:50:39.630 --> 00:50:41.596
like don't compare your pain to my pain.

00:50:41.596 --> 00:50:44.720
Like, there's a lot of animosity

00:50:44.720 --> 00:50:48.305
when there's really a
lot of shared history

00:50:48.305 --> 00:50:53.173
and shared goals really.

00:50:54.730 --> 00:50:58.860
So yes, that's been a large
part of my faith journey

00:50:58.860 --> 00:51:02.443
and it's informed all
of it in different ways.

00:51:03.380 --> 00:51:04.213
- Very encouraging to hear.

00:51:04.213 --> 00:51:06.040
And there's great perspective as well.

00:51:06.040 --> 00:51:08.198
I mean, both your journey
and the story that you shared

00:51:08.198 --> 00:51:10.590
of the individual just
that aspect of grace

00:51:10.590 --> 00:51:15.590
and forgiveness is tremendous, tremendous.

00:51:15.590 --> 00:51:16.650
Well, thank you so much.

00:51:16.650 --> 00:51:18.180
We've covered a lot of different topics.

00:51:18.180 --> 00:51:21.050
I really enjoy just having
this free flowing conversation.

00:51:21.050 --> 00:51:23.370
And just, again, all the
work that you've done

00:51:23.370 --> 00:51:25.780
to bring so many important stories to life

00:51:25.780 --> 00:51:27.040
and to larger audiences,

00:51:27.040 --> 00:51:29.810
and hopefully to help us
move forward as a society

00:51:29.810 --> 00:51:31.610
to a better place as well.

00:51:31.610 --> 00:51:33.910
Definitely appreciate you
taking the time to be here

00:51:33.910 --> 00:51:36.010
to share a little bit about your journey

00:51:36.010 --> 00:51:37.667
and about the path that you place

00:51:37.667 --> 00:51:40.540
and the ways that you're
speaking up on behalf of so many.

00:51:40.540 --> 00:51:41.650
Thank you so much for taking time

00:51:41.650 --> 00:51:43.640
to be here on the Addy Hour.

00:51:43.640 --> 00:51:46.700
- Thank you, Dr. Addy, and
I am a huge fan of your work

00:51:46.700 --> 00:51:47.670
and wanna dig into it.

00:51:47.670 --> 00:51:49.053
So someday I could interview you.

00:51:49.053 --> 00:51:51.030
- That would be wonderful.

00:51:51.030 --> 00:51:52.050
Thank you so much.

00:51:52.050 --> 00:51:54.070
I appreciate the investment

00:51:54.070 --> 00:51:55.690
and I may take you up
on that offer as well.

00:51:55.690 --> 00:51:58.020
I think our listeners would
definitely enjoy that.

00:51:58.020 --> 00:51:59.580
- I know they would.

00:51:59.580 --> 00:52:01.670
- Excellent. Thanks so much.

00:52:01.670 --> 00:52:03.097
- All right, bye.

00:52:03.097 --> 00:52:05.680
